      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 9.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 25,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.23244_p3_b21",
      "text": "Pilanci & Ergen (2020) have shown (2) admits a convex\nreformulation, thus alleviating the inherent difficulties of\nthe non-convex landscape in deep learning. When condition\nm \u2265m\u2217is satisfied for some m \u2265n + 1, the reformula-\ntion has the same optimal value as the original non-convex\nproblem and no information is lost.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.44,
        88.05,
        543.09,
        157.86
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        1229,
        352,
        2172,
        631
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 319,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.23244_p3_b22",
      "text": "The convex reformulation is based on enumerating the ac-\ntions of all possible ReLU activation patterns on the data\nmatrix X. These activation patterns act as separating hy-\nperplanes, which essentially multiply the rows of X by 0\nor 1 and can be represented by diagonal matrices. For fixed\nX, the set of all possible ReLU activation patterns may be\nexpressed as\n",
      "bbox": [
